  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Hot and fairly muggy through the weekend.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nHotter today with highs around 90 for most areas. Similar\ntemperatures for Thursday, then slightly less hot Friday through\nSunday in the north, but just about as hot for the Bangor region\nand Downeast. Today and Thursday will be somewhat muggy but not\ntoo bad. However, quite a bit muggier Friday into the weekend\nwith dewpoints nearing 70. So although it may be a touch less\nhot Friday to Sunday, it will feel just as hot due to higher\ndewpoints. At some point, we could approach heat advisory\ncriteria of heat indices of 95, most likely on Friday and in the\nBangor region. As mugginess increases, nighttime lows are quite\nmild from Thursday night into the weekend. There will be a bit\nof relief from the heat near the coast mainly as afternoon sea \nbreezes, though any of the days could get quite hot before the\nsea breeze kicks in.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Scattered showers and thunderstorms late this \nweek. Some severe storms are possible.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\nLow pressure system approaches from the northwest, moving into\nNew Brunswick. The timing of the cold front is still uncertain,\nbut for this update, the front should enter the region Friday\nnight and move through on Saturday. Both Friday and Saturday\nhave ample instability with CAPE values above 1000 J/kg,\nsteepening lapse rates, and plenty of upper level divergence.\nHowever, models are trending to a less active day on Friday with\nmore pulse isolated storms due to the timing of the front. By\nSaturday, the low intensifies with more moisture moving into the\narea. Upper air model soundings still show long skinny CAPE on\nboth Friday and Saturday, but now Saturday has a much more moist\ncolumn compared to Friday. In addition, higher PWAT values favor\nSaturday for more rainfall. Nevertheless, the weekend looks to\nbe active and more model updates are needed to pin down the\ntiming of the front.",
